    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Cancer stem cells (CSCs) are essential for the growth and proliferation of some tumors, and therapies aimed at clearance of CSCs have potential as treatments, and possibly cures, of these cancers. Here we demonstrate that the cell surface antigen CD90 (Thy-1), which has been previously established as a marker of neoplastic stem cells, can be targeted by monoclonal anti-CD90 antibodies to modulate tumor growth. Anti-mouse and anti-human monoclonal CD90 antibodies were used to evaluate the potential of CD90 as a therapeutic antibody target in mouse breast, human bladder, and human breast cancers engrafted in mice. In treating transplanted mice with anti-CD90 antibodies, we found that anti-CD90 antibodies accelerated mouse breast tumor growth. Simultaneously, there was a decrease in T cell frequency when transplanted mice were treated with anti-mouse CD90 antibodies. However, bone marrow transplant and adoptive T cell transfer experiments revealed that hematopoietic cells or T cells did not mediate the increase in tumor growth. In the xenotransplantation models, we discovered that anti-CD90 antibodies inhibit growth of human bladder tumors engrafted in immunodeficient mice in a preliminary experiment, but in a second experiment, the antibodies had no significant effect on tumor growth. Anti-CD90 antibodies also had no significant effect on the growth of human breast cancer cells. These results indicate that in some tumors, antibody targeting of CD90 modulates proliferation or death of tumor cells via activating/blocking downstream CD90 signaling or affecting anti-cancer immune response. It may be possible to manipulate the CD90 signaling pathway or anti-CD90-mediated immune mechanism so as to promote or inhibit tumor growth. These findings can lead to the development of therapeutic agents that control CD90 signaling to inhibit tumor development, progression, and/or relapse.",
